  'help.ai.body': '<ul><li><b>Objects</b>: recognizes 80 everyday things (person, cup, ball, book…). Blocks give whether it is visible, its X/Y position, size and confidence.</li><li><b>Teach objects</b>: show something to the camera and hold <b>📸</b> to collect pictures (up to 5 items, 20+ pictures each). Add one item with only the background. The learning is saved in this browser; use Export/Import to move it to another computer.</li><li><b>Colors</b>: reads the color in the center square. To track a color, put it in the square and press <b>Learn</b> (up to 5). Blocks give whether it is visible, its X/Y and its size.</li><li>Turning on many modes at once makes each one slower. Turn on only what your program uses.</li></ul>',
  "dl.esp32": "ESP32-side files (.zip)",
  "dl.esp32Desc": "main.py for the LMS-ESP32 (made from your blocks) + lpf2.py, pupremote.py",
  "dl.esp32Readme": "How to copy to the LMS-ESP32 (Thonny IDE)\n1) Install Thonny from thonny.org and connect the ESP32 by USB.\n2) In the bottom-right corner (or Tools → Options → Interpreter) choose MicroPython (ESP32) and the port. When the shell shows >>> you are connected (if text keeps scrolling, press the red Stop button).\n3) View → Files. In \"This computer\" select main.py, lpf2.py and pupremote.py (plus rm_i2cdev.py and rm_vl53.py for sensor blocks) from this folder, right-click → \"Upload to /\".\n4) Always restart: press Ctrl+D in the shell (soft reset) or the reset button. When the shell shows \"lpf2 2.1.3\" the new files are running.\n5) Plug the ESP32 into a hub port and press ▶ Run in the editor after 2–3 seconds.\n* Pybricks 4.x hubs need lpf2.py 2.1 or newer (2.1.3 in this folder). The copied files are used instead of the older ones built into the ESP32 firmware.\n* Instead of Thonny you can also use ViperIDE (viper-ide.org, in Chrome).\npupremote.py, lpf2.py: AntonsMindstorms.com (GPL-3.0)",
  "esp.gen.head": "LMS-ESP32 main.py — hub port {0} — generated from your blocks by RoboMakers WordBlock",
  "esp.gen.copy": "Copy this file with lpf2.py and pupremote.py to the ESP32 with Thonny IDE (View → Files → right-click \"Upload to /\").",
  "esp.gen.reset": "Afterwards always press Ctrl+D (soft reset) or the reset button → run on the hub after 2–3 s. Order, names and formats must match the hub blocks.",
  "esp.gen.args": "Values sent by the hub (format {0}): args",
  "esp.gen.ret": "Values returned to the hub (format {0})",
  "esp.gen.todo": "TODO: replace with real sensor values",
  "esp.gen.long": "⚠ \"{0}\" is longer than 5 characters. With power=True only 5 or fewer work — rename it here and in the hub block.",
  "hint.esp32": "💡 The hub could not find the ESP32 on port {0}. Please check:\n  1) Does the ESP32 have a main.py using PUPRemoteSensor, and is it running? (⬇ Download → \"ESP32-side files\")\n  2) Pybricks 4.x hubs need lpf2.py 2.1 or newer on the ESP32. Copy lpf2.py and pupremote.py from the same zip to the ESP32.\n  3) Reset the ESP32 and run again after 2–3 seconds.\n  4) Is the cable in hub port {0}, and is the block port also {0}?",
  "hint.bluepad": "💡 The hub could not find the LMS-ESP32 (BluePad32) on port {0}.\n  1) Is the latest \"BluePad32 LPF2 for Pybricks\" firmware on the ESP32? (firmware.antonsmindstorms.com)\n  2) Reset the ESP32 and run again after 2–3 seconds.\n  3) Is the cable in hub port {0}, and is the block port also {0}?",
  "hint.husky": "💡 The hub could not find the HuskyLens bridge (ESP32) on port {0}.\n  1) Is the husky_spike_esp32 program on the bridge ESP32 and running?\n  2) Reset the bridge and run again after 2–3 seconds.\n  3) Is the cable in hub port {0}, and is the block port also {0}?",
  "help.esp32.title": "📡 Connecting an LMS-ESP32 (PUPRemote)",
  "help.esp32.body": "<ol><li>Add <b>ESP32 channel/command</b> or <b>ESP32 pin</b> blocks (servo, NeoPixel …) — see the \"ESP32\" example.</li><li>Use <b>⬇ Download → ESP32-side files</b> and unzip it. The files to flash to the ESP32 are <b>main.py · lpf2.py · pupremote.py</b>. main.py is made from your blocks and already contains the pin-block code.</li><li><b>Copy them to the ESP32 with Thonny IDE</b><ol type=\"a\"><li>Install Thonny from <a href=\"https://thonny.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">thonny.org</a> and connect the ESP32 to the computer with a USB cable.</li><li>In the bottom-right corner (or Tools → Options → Interpreter) choose <b>MicroPython (ESP32)</b> and the port (COM… or /dev/cu.…). When the shell shows <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> you are connected. If text keeps scrolling, press the red <b>Stop</b> button.</li><li>Open View → <b>Files</b>. In \"This computer\" select the 3 files, right-click → <b>\"Upload to /\"</b>. Files with the same name are overwritten.</li><li><b>Always restart the ESP32</b>: press <b>Ctrl+D</b> in the shell (soft reset) or the board's reset button. When the shell shows <code>lpf2 2.1.3</code> the new files are running. Without a restart the old program keeps running and the hub shows <code>ENODEV</code>.</li></ol></li><li>Plug the ESP32 into a hub port and press ▶ Run after 2–3 seconds. When you change the blocks, download main.py again, copy it the same way and restart.</li><li>Pybricks <b>4.x</b> hubs need <b>lpf2.py 2.1 or newer</b> on the ESP32 (2.1.3 is in the zip). Names: up to 5 letters; order and formats must match on both sides. The hub link pins (usually GPIO 18 and 19) cannot be used. On a Chromebook without Thonny you can use <a href=\"https://viper-ide.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ViperIDE</a> (runs in Chrome) the same way.</li></ol>",
