  "esp.gen.io": "RoboMakers I/O commands — keep these 10 lines first, in this order.",
  "hint.esp32mode": "💡 The hub and the ESP32 registered different things (names, order or formats). Use ⬇ Download → \"ESP32-side files\" again, copy main.py to the ESP32, reset it and run again.",
  "tab.husky": "HuskyLens",
  "tab.learn": "Learn",
  "learn.line.name": "Line following, step by step",
  "learn.line.intro": "Build a robot that follows a black line in four steps. Each step adds a few blocks, and <b>new blocks glow yellow</b>.<br>Robot: wheel motors {0}·{1}, colour sensor {2} looking at the floor. The robot follows the <b>left edge of the black line</b>.",
  "learn.line.1.short": "On-off",
  "learn.line.1.title": "Step 1 · On-off control",
  "learn.line.1.idea": "The colour sensor reads how bright the floor is, from 0 to 100 (<b>reflection</b>). On the black line it is about 10, on white about 90, and half over the edge somewhere in between.<br>On-off control asks just one question: <b>“Is it dark right now?”</b> If it is dark (less than 50), the robot is too far onto the line, so it turns left. If it is bright, it has left the line, so it turns right.<br>With only two actions, the robot <b>zigzags</b> along the line.",
  "learn.line.1.change": "Drive setup, forever loop, “if reflection &lt; 50 drive turning left, else drive turning right”",
  "learn.line.1.try": "• Raise the speed from 120 to 200. Does it lose the line on curves?<br>• Change the turn values 60·−60 to 30 or 100. How does the wobbling change?",
  "learn.line.2.short": "P control",
  "learn.line.2.title": "Step 2 · P control (proportional)",
  "learn.line.2.idea": "On-off turns just as hard whether the robot is a little or a lot off the line. When you ride a bike, you steer a little for a small mistake and a lot for a big one.<br><b>error = reflection − target (50)</b>. Exactly on the edge it is 0, inside the line (dark) it is −, outside (bright) it is +.<br><b>steer = Kp × error</b>. The robot steers in <b>proportion</b> to the error, so this is P control. Kp sets how strongly it reacts.",
  "learn.line.2.change": "The “if” block is gone, and the variables <b>target · Kp · error · steer</b> appear. The drive block now turns by the “steer” variable.",
  "learn.line.2.try": "• Try Kp = 0.5 → 1.2 → 3. Too small and it cannot make the curve, too big and it swings left and right.<br>• Print the reflection to the terminal and put the value halfway between black and white into target.",
  "learn.line.3.short": "PD control",
  "learn.line.3.title": "Step 3 · PD control (proportional + derivative)",
  "learn.line.3.idea": "With P control the robot comes back to the line with speed, <b>overshoots</b> it, steers back the other way… and keeps wobbling.<br><b>derivative = error − last error</b>: how fast the error changes in one loop. When the robot rushes toward the line, the derivative has the opposite sign and <b>brakes early</b>.<br><b>steer = Kp × error + Kd × derivative</b>. At the end of the loop, “last error = error” remembers the current error.",
  "learn.line.3.change": "Variables <b>Kd · last_error · derivative</b>, “derivative = error − last_error”, “+ Kd × derivative” in steer, and “last_error = error” at the end of the loop",
  "learn.line.3.try": "• With Kd = 0 it is the same as step 2 (P). Compare the wobbling with 0 → 4 → 10.<br>• Raise the speed to 200. Does it follow better than P control?",
  "learn.line.4.short": "PID control",
  "learn.line.4.title": "Step 4 · PID control (proportional + integral + derivative)",
  "learn.line.4.idea": "On a long curve a small error can <b>stay on one side</b>. P and D alone find it hard to remove that last small error.<br><b>integral = all the errors added up so far</b>. Even a small error grows when it lasts, and pushes the robot back toward the line.<br><b>steer = Kp × error + Ki × integral + Kd × derivative</b>. If the integral grows too big, it later steers too hard the other way (wind-up), so we limit it to −1000 … 1000.",
  "learn.line.4.change": "Variables <b>Ki · integral</b>, “integral = (integral + error) limited to −1000 … 1000”, and “+ Ki × integral” in steer",
  "learn.line.4.try": "• With Ki = 0 it is the same as step 3 (PD). Try 0.02 → 0.1. Too big and it slowly swings wide.<br>• Like in a competition, raise the speed and adjust Kp → Kd → Ki one at a time. This is called <b>tuning</b>.",
  "learn.ideaTitle": "💡 How it works",
  "learn.changeTitle": "🧩 Blocks changed in this step",
  "learn.tryTitle": "🔬 Try it",
  "learn.load": "📥 Load this step’s blocks",
  "learn.show": "✨ Show new blocks",
  "learn.sim": "🧪 Try in the simulator",
  "learn.prev": "◀ Previous step",
  "learn.next": "Next step ▶",
  "learn.loaded": "Step {0} blocks loaded. The glowing yellow blocks are the new ones.",
  "learn.line.noHub": "SPIKE Essential has only 2 ports, so two wheel motors and a colour sensor cannot all be connected. Choose SPIKE Prime or EV3 as the hub at the top.",
  "learn.line.simNote": "🧪 The simulator picks the “Line tracing” mat automatically. Its sensor sees a sharp line edge, so the differences between steps may look small there. They show more clearly on a real robot and floor.",
