  "learn.gyro.name": "Gyro driving, step by step",
  "learn.gyro.intro": "Learn in seven steps how the gyro sensor inside the hub makes the robot <b>drive straight and turn accurately</b>. New blocks glow yellow.<br>Robot: wheel motors {0}·{1} (wheel 56 mm, track 112 mm — change them to match your robot).",
  "learn.gyro.noHub": "The EV3 brick has no built-in gyro. Choose SPIKE Prime or SPIKE Essential as the hub at the top.",
  "learn.gyro.simNote": "🧪 The simulator opens with the “Grid” mat, so you can see how far the robot drifts. A real robot drifts more because of wheel slip and the battery.",
  "learn.gyro.1.short": "Heading",
  "learn.gyro.1.title": "Step 1 · Measuring heading (yaw)",
  "learn.gyro.1.idea": "The gyro sensor inside the hub measures <b>how far the robot has turned</b>. This value is the <b>heading (yaw)</b>.<br>Reset the heading to 0 at the start, and that direction becomes 0°. <b>Turning clockwise is +</b>, the other way is −.<br>It keeps <b>adding up</b> past one turn: 360 → 720 … So targets like 270° or 720° work as they are.",
  "learn.gyro.1.change": "“Reset hub heading to 0”, forever “print heading to terminal → wait 0.2 s”",
  "learn.gyro.1.try": "• Slowly turn the robot clockwise by hand, one full turn. Is it close to 360?<br>• What happens the other way? With two turns?<br>• If one exact turn shows 357 or 362, that is the small <b>sensor error</b> each hub has.",
  "learn.gyro.2.short": "Wheels only",
  "learn.gyro.2.title": "Step 2 · Driving straight with wheels only",
  "learn.gyro.2.idea": "The “drive straight 600 mm” block keeps the <b>angles of both wheels</b> equal. But if a wheel slips a little or the floor is uneven, the robot <b>slowly curves</b> even though the wheels turned the same.<br>So we print the heading at the end. If it is not 0, the robot turned that much.",
  "learn.gyro.2.change": "Drive setup (gyro off), “drive straight 600 mm”, and “print heading” at the end",
  "learn.gyro.2.try": "• Run it three times and write down the heading. Is it the same each time?<br>• Does it change on another floor (desk, mat, carpet)?",
  "learn.gyro.3.short": "P straight",
  "learn.gyro.3.title": "Step 3 · Gyro P straight",
  "learn.gyro.3.idea": "Now the robot looks at its <b>heading</b> instead of the wheels and keeps itself straight.<br><b>error = target_heading − heading</b>. If it drifts right (heading +), the error is −.<br><b>steer = Kp × error</b>. A − error steers left, a + error steers right, back to the target heading — the same idea as P control in line following.<br>It repeats every 0.01 s until the distance reaches 600 mm. Distance is the <b>average of both wheels</b>, more accurate than one wheel.",
  "learn.gyro.3.change": "“Reset drive distance and angle”, variables <b>target_heading · Kp · speed · error · steer</b>, “repeat while distance &lt; 600” with the error and steer calculation and “keep driving”, and “stop driving” at the end",
  "learn.gyro.3.try": "• Try Kp = 0.5 · 2 · 8. Big values wobble, small ones correct slowly.<br>• Nudge the robot by hand while it drives. Does it come back to its heading?<br>• What if target_heading is 10?",
  "learn.gyro.4.short": "PI straight",
  "learn.gyro.4.title": "Step 4 · Gyro PI straight (+ integral)",
  "learn.gyro.4.idea": "If the robot keeps pulling to one side (uneven motors, weight), P alone <b>leaves a small error</b>: a small error gives only a small steer, so it never fully corrects.<br><b>integral = all the errors added up</b>. Even a small error grows if it lasts, and corrects harder.<br><b>steer = Kp × error + Ki × integral</b>. If the robot is blocked for a long time (say, rubbing a wall), the integral would grow forever, so we <b>limit it to −200 … 200</b>.",
  "learn.gyro.4.change": "Variables <b>Ki · integral</b>, “integral = (integral + error) limited to −200 … 200”, and “+ Ki × integral” in steer",
  "learn.gyro.4.try": "• With Ki = 0 it is the same as step 3. Try 0.05 → 0.2.<br>• Wrap tape around one wheel so the robot pulls to one side, and compare P with PI.",
  "learn.gyro.5.short": "Speed plan",
  "learn.gyro.5.title": "Step 5 · Trapezoid speed (start slow · arrive slow)",
  "learn.gyro.5.idea": "Starting fast makes wheels slip, and stopping from full speed makes the robot <b>slide too far</b>. So the speed follows a <b>trapezoid</b>: start slow → speed up → top speed → slow down near the end.<br>Start side = min_speed + accel × traveled, arrival side = min_speed + accel × remaining. Take the <b>slower of the two</b> and keep it between min and max speed.<br>“The slower one” matters: if the slowdown always started from max_speed, the speed would <b>jump up</b> when the robot had not reached max_speed yet (dashed line).",
  "learn.gyro.5.change": "Variables <b>length · max_speed · min_speed · accel · traveled · remaining</b>, speed calculation and “if the arrival side is slower” at the start of the loop; “speed = 200” is gone",
  "learn.gyro.5.try": "• Try accel = 0.5 · 1.5 · 5. How do the start and stop change?<br>• Does it still stop exactly at 600 mm with max_speed 500?<br>• With length 200 it never reaches max_speed. Does the speed still not jump?",
  "learn.gyro.6.short": "Gyro turn",
  "learn.gyro.6.title": "Step 6 · Gyro turn (stop inside the tolerance)",
  "learn.gyro.6.idea": "After driving straight, the robot spins in place to a <b>target heading of 90°</b>.<br>It turns in proportion to the angle left (error), so it <b>slows down as it gets close</b>: turn = Kt × error, limited to −200 … 200 °/s.<br>When the error is inside the <b>tolerance (±1°)</b> it stops, waits 0.3 s until it is completely still, then checks the heading — a fast turn keeps going a little after stopping.",
  "learn.gyro.6.change": "After the straight: “target_heading = 90”, variables <b>Kt · tolerance</b>, “repeat until −tolerance &lt; error and error &lt; tolerance” (spin in place and recalculate the error), “stop driving”, “wait 0.3 s”",
  "learn.gyro.6.try": "• Try Kt = 1 · 3 · 10. Big values overshoot and come back, small ones take long.<br>• What about tolerance 0.2? Too small and it may wiggle at the end for a long time.<br>• Try target_heading −90 · 180 · 450.",
  "learn.gyro.7.short": "Pybricks gyro",
  "learn.gyro.7.title": "Step 7 · Pybricks gyro driving",
  "learn.gyro.7.idea": "Everything you built — heading correction, trapezoid speed, stopping inside a tolerance — Pybricks’ DriveBase <b>does inside</b>. Tick <b>“gyro”</b> in drive setup, and the straight and turn blocks use the gyro.<br>The program is much shorter, but the ideas are the same. Teams usually drive like this in competitions and write their own code only for special moves.",
  "learn.gyro.7.change": "The step 6 blocks are gone: drive setup with <b>gyro on</b>, “set drive speed 300, turn speed 150”, “drive straight 600 mm”, “turn 90°”",
  "learn.gyro.7.try": "• Compare the final heading of step 2 (wheels only) and step 7.<br>• Untick “gyro” and run again. What changes?<br>• Raise the speed to 500 and compare the accuracy with your step 6 code.",
  "learn.chart.cw": "clockwise = +",
